{
 "family": "identity",
 "note": "The rules of the identity changes of leg 6-identity. Ruling I1 (op user-create, CheckUserCreate): a new local user has an id of the user pattern (R1 with @ and +, 256 characters), an id that no user of any source and no role mapping of the local source holds (409), known roles, a known status (Q7) and a password (I5); the 400 refusals travel together in the order roles, status, password. Ruling I5 (op password, CheckPassword): a password is not empty, has at most 1024 bytes and holds no control character; there is no minimum length. The key repeat repeats the password that many times. Ruling I4 (op role-in-use, CheckRoleInUse): the delete of a role is refused with 409 while a user, a role mapping, a containing role, the anonymous user or the default role of the realms holds it, and the message names the first holder in this order by kind and id. The row or the mapping of the user that anonymous access names counts as the anonymous user, not as a user or a mapping. Ruling S22 (ops role-create and role-save, CheckRoleCreate and CheckRoleSave): the id of a new role is in the name pattern and free (409), a save keeps its id, a built in role does not change (400 at *), and every privilege of the role exists, stored, built in or generated; a privilege the stored role holds is never refused (R6). The rules of R5 hold as in roles.json. Ruling I7 (op user-save, SaveUser): a save of a stored user keeps its id; a local user takes names, mail, status (Q7) and roles from the body; a user of another source keeps names, mail and status, and a change of them is refused with 409 (Q6); a save of the own user keeps its roles unless the subject may administer users. The key result holds the record after the save. Ruling S25 (op anonymous, CheckAnonymous): userId is a user id of the pattern, realmName a realm this server builds; the stored values of the setting below are never refused (R6). Ruling R9 (op anonymous-roles, CheckAnonymousRoles): roles of anonymous access do not replace the roles of a local user that signs in with a password; the key hashed of the file names the users that have a stored password. Ruling N16 (op realms, CheckRealmList and CheckRealmRole): the list of active realms is not empty, holds no empty, unknown or repeated id, and names DefaultRole only with a default role; refusals at *. Ruling I10 (op anonymous-user, CheckAnonymousUser): the user anonymous access names does not sign in: no local user with a stored password, whatever its status, and no user of a directory source, a stored row or a role mapping, matched without regard to letter case (409 anonymous-sign-in); a save that turns anonymous access off and keeps the stored user is not refused. The key stored replaces the stored setting of the file. Ruling I9 (op grant, CheckGrant): a change gives a role or a user no permission the acting subject does not hold: every permission the roles expand to after the change, contained roles included, is implied by a permission of the subject (key held), else 403 grant-exceeds; a subject that holds nexus:* passes. The key role is stored before the check, in place of a role of its id; the key what names the record in the message. A role named like a directory group counts the same, and a password change counts as a grant of the permissions of its user. Ruling I11 (op realm-administrators, CheckRealmAdministrators): while an active local user holds the full permission set (A10), the active realms keep NexusAuthenticatingRealm, else 409 last-administrator; the administrators of A10 are local users, so that realm is the one they sign in through. The key user replaces the user of its id in the file. Ruling I12 (op user-realm, UserSource): the query realm of a user delete is a realm id as the interface sends it, NexusAuthenticatingRealm for the local source default and LdapRealm for the source LDAP, or a source name; absent it is default; any other value is 400 realm-invalid at realm. The key source is the source the realm names. Ruling I15 (op default-role-grant, CheckDefaultRoleGrant): an order of active realms that makes DefaultRole active, which the stored order (key storedRealms) does not hold, gives the stored default role to every signed in user: each permission it expands to, contained roles included, is implied by a permission of the subject (key held), else 403 grant-exceeds. An order that keeps an active DefaultRole is not checked (R6). Ruling I16 (op anonymous-grant, CheckAnonymousGrant): a save that turns anonymous access on gives requests without credentials the rights of the user it names, its stored local row whatever its status, else the role mappings of the local source matched without regard to letter case: each permission its roles expand to is implied by a permission of the subject (key held), else 403 grant-exceeds. A save that turns access off is not checked.",
